Wokwi black-box smoke test
==========================

From the repository root, build the Wokwi fixture and run the smoke harness:

    pio run --project-dir NetworkManager -e wokwi
    bash NetworkManager/tools/wokwi_smoke.sh

The CLI smoke is serial-only because Wokwi CI runs the simulation remotely.
For HTTP/API assertions, start the project with Wokwi for VS Code and its
Private IoT Gateway, then run:

    bash NetworkManager/tools/wokwi_http_smoke.sh

The `[[net.forward]]` entry in `NetworkManager/wokwi.toml` is used by the VS
Code integration to expose `localhost:8180`; it is not a Wokwi CLI port
forward.

The smoke harness and ad hoc Wokwi commands use
`NetworkManager/tools/wokwi_cli.sh`. It reads `NetworkManager/.env.local` when
`WOKWI_CLI_TOKEN` is not already exported. The file must contain only a single
`WOKWI_CLI_TOKEN=...` assignment, be a regular non-symlink file, and have
owner-only permissions (`chmod 600`). The loader never sources shell code or
prints the token.

The fixture credentials come from `[sim] api_login` and `api_password` in
`NetworkManager/ini/user_config.ini`. They are injected into the `wokwi` build
only, assigned in RAM after configuration load, and are not included in
release/OTA environments. The initial assignment is volatile; normal API/OTA
save behavior may persist the current configuration. The harness does not
create credential files or perform OTA uploads. Compiler errors and other
build failures are outside the scope of this smoke harness.

This directory is intended for PlatformIO Test Runner and project tests.

Persistence recovery remains a hardware seam: Preferences/NVS and ESP8266
LittleFS corruption, replacement, and power-loss behavior are not executable
in the current hostless test directory and require board-level validation.

Unit Testing is a software testing method by which individual units of
source code, sets of one or more MCU program modules together with associated
control data, usage procedures, and operating procedures, are tested to
determine whether they are fit for use. Unit testing finds problems early
in the development cycle.

More information about PlatformIO Unit Testing:
- https://docs.platformio.org/en/latest/advanced/unit-testing/index.html
